/**
 * Scene for "The journey of a commit to GitHub and back" (lesson
 * git/bajar-y-subir-cambios, order: 4), a short film played on one persistent
 * motion canvas. The captions and cues live in
 * `src/data/animations/sincronizacion-remota-git.ts`.
 *
 * The canvas has two halves. The editor on the left is a fixed overlay: it is the
 * terminal session of the lesson, one command per line, and the cues type each
 * command, slide a bar onto it and type its real output below. The viewport on
 * the right holds the camera: three isometric platforms, Ana's computer, the
 * remote on GitHub (`origin`) and Beto's computer. A commit is always the same
 * rounded node with its short hash; a copy of it travels along the pipes on
 * every push, clone, fetch and pull. `main` is a solid tag and `origin/main` a
 * dashed one (a snapshot of the remote). Every command has a module on the
 * platform of the person who runs it, labeled with its editor line. Magenta marks
 * the problem (the rejected push), violet what resolves it (the merge commit).
 *
 * Palette: films are the approved exception to the Organic palette (DESIGN.md §8).
 * The colors are the `--xj-*` tokens declared once in the style block; nothing
 * else in the file uses a raw color.
 */
